menu "espOS OTA"

    config ESPOS_OTA_MANIFEST_MAX
        int "Largest manifest accepted (bytes)"
        default 16384
        range 1024 65536
        help
            The version manifest (docs/ota.md) is fetched whole into RAM
            before it is parsed; a longer body fails the check. 16 KiB holds
            a manifest with dozens of builds. Raise it for a channel that
            lists every target of a long release history -- it costs that
            much heap only while a check runs.

    config ESPOS_OTA_BOOT_CHECK_DELAY_S
        int "First automatic manifest check after boot (s)"
        default 20
        range 0 3600
        help
            Uptime the automatic check waits for before its first fetch. It
            also waits for WiFi to be connected, so this is a settling time
            rather than a timeout: DHCP, mDNS and SignalK discovery all run
            in those first seconds, and a manifest fetch (or the install it
            may trigger) competing with them slows every one of them down.

    config ESPOS_OTA_NO_STA_GRACE_S
        int "Confirm a new image without a station config after (s)"
        default 60
        range 10 3600
        help
            A freshly installed image confirms itself once WiFi is connected.
            A device with no station configured (portal only) never gets
            there, so it is confirmed after this much uptime instead -- long
            enough that an image which crashes at boot has already been
            rolled back by the bootloader. Must be below ota.confirm_tmo_s,
            or the rollback timeout wins first.

    config ESPOS_OTA_STACK_SIZE
        int "OTA task stack size (bytes)"
        default 8192
        range 4096 32768
        help
            The task that fetches the manifest and runs esp_https_ota. TLS
            handshakes run on it, which is what the 8 KiB is for. The linux
            simulator sizes its task in words and ignores this.

endmenu
